# The step of dot_2_entry, the dot product of two limbs of arrays the program supplies: dot_step_2,
# p = mul_2_2(x, y); s = add_2_2(s, p), on the limbs of the elements guarded (rule R3, the factors
# of a fused operation: architecture.md). dot_step_2 only multiplies its elements, which R3 alone
# does not guard; but mul_2_2 takes them as factors of fused operations whose addends are not zero,
# and a compiler that sees the other factor to be 1 rewrites fma(x, 1, s) as x + s and may fuse a
# product the program stored into the array into that addition (seen in the dot product of one
# limb, not measured in this one). After the guards each limb is a number of the format, the
# rounded product at most. Its claims are those of dot_step_2: the guard is the identity on numbers
# of the format.
kernel:        dot_entry_step_2
input:         s = s0 s1
input:         supplied x = x0 x1
input:         supplied y = y0 y1
output:        t = t0 t1
value:         s+x*y
requires:      normalized s
requires:      normalized x
requires:      normalized y
ensures:       normalized t
ensures:       error t T c=3*(1+4*u*u)/(1-4*u)+4 k=2 t=2
min_precision: 4
theorem:       proofs/theories/StepKernels.v dot_entry_step_2_correct
ieee_bridge_theorem: proofs/theories/IeeeSteps.v dot_entry_step_2_ieee_bridge
ieee_spec_theorem: proofs/theories/IeeeSteps.v dot_entry_step_2_ieee_correct
ieee_lead_theorem: proofs/theories/IeeeFolds.v dot_entry_step_2_ieee_lead
source:        dot_step_2 on the guarded limbs of the elements (rule R3, the factors of a fused operation); bounds from MR22
license:       algorithm: published; proof: MIT
status:        proved
entry:         yes
ieee:          bridge spec

gx0      = guard(x0)
gx1      = guard(x1)
gy0      = guard(y0)
gy1      = guard(y1)
(t0, t1) = dot_step_2(s0, s1, gx0, gx1, gy0, gy1)
